page('company','Company','The engineering philosophy behind the fictional Veylan Aerospace concept.','''
<section class="company-intro wrap"><h1>Aircraft for testing<br>new technology.</h1><p class="lead">Veylan builds reusable uncrewed test aircraft for sensor manufacturers, aerospace development teams and research organisations. Our offer combines Sable with equipment integration support and test preparation.</p></section>
<figure class="company-image"><img src="/assets/wing.webp" alt="Aircraft wing and engines in a maintenance facility" width="1800" height="1199" fetchpriority="high"></figure>
<section class="statement wrap"><h2>Engineering doesn’t<br>end at takeoff.</h2><div><p class="lead">An aircraft needs to do more than fly. It needs to be understood, inspected and supported by the people working with it.</p><p>That is the idea behind Veylan. Aircraft design and systems development belong in the same conversation as maintenance, integration and the next day’s work in the hangar.</p><p>By looking at the complete development cycle, we can ask better questions at the start of a programme—and make each test more useful.</p></div></section>
<section class="philosophy wrap"><h2>A practical approach<br>to ambitious work.</h2><div class="philosophy-grid"><article><h3>Design for access</h3><p>The people who integrate, inspect and service a platform should have a voice in how it is designed. Maintainability starts before the first part is made.</p></article><article><h3>Make the test count</h3><p>Begin with a clear engineering question. Define what needs to be observed and how the results will guide the next decision.</p></article><article><h3>Consider the whole system</h3><p>Aircraft, onboard equipment, software and ground processes depend on one another. Evaluate them together rather than treating integration as an afterthought.</p></article><article><h3>Leave room to change</h3><p>Development programmes evolve. A platform should make configuration changes understandable and give teams a way to build on what they have learned.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="company-product"><div><h2>Meet the platform<br>behind the approach.</h2><p>Sable gives your team an aircraft to carry test equipment, recover it after flight and prepare a revised configuration.</p><a class="button light" href="/sable/">Explore Sable</a></div><div class="company-mark" aria-hidden="true">V</div></section>
<section class="closing wrap"><h2>Tell us what<br>you want to test.</h2><div><p>Tell us about your equipment, the results you need to measure and where you are in development.</p><a class="button" href="/contact/">Discuss your test programme</a></div></section>''')
page('contact','Discuss your test programme','Discuss equipment integration, test objectives and programme readiness for Sable.','''
<section class="contact-layout wrap"><div><h1>What do you<br>want to test?</h1><p class="lead">Tell us about your equipment and the results you need. Start with what you know; detailed integration requirements can follow.</p></div><form id="enquiry"><div class="form-pair"><label>Your name<input name="name" autocomplete="name" required></label><label>Organisation<input name="company" autocomplete="organization" required></label></div><label>Work email<input name="email" type="email" autocomplete="email" required></label><label>What equipment do you want to test?<select name="equipment" required><option value="">Select equipment</option><option value="camera">Camera or sensor</option><option value="software">Flight software or onboard computer</option><option value="radio">Radio or communications equipment</option><option value="other">Other equipment</option></select></label><label>Describe the equipment<input name="equipmentDescription" placeholder="For example, a camera and its recording unit" required></label><label>What do you need to evaluate?<textarea name="objectives" rows="4" placeholder="Tell us what the flight needs to test and what you want to measure." required></textarea></label><div class="form-pair"><label>Programme stage<select name="stage" required><option value="">Select a stage</option><option>Early research</option><option>Bench-tested prototype</option><option>Preparing for flight testing</option><option>Existing flight-test programme</option></select></label><label>What support do you need?<select name="support" required><option>Sable aircraft and integration support</option><option>Sable aircraft</option><option>Equipment integration and test preparation</option><option>I’d like to discuss the options</option></select></label></div><div class="contact-note"><h2>Preview this enquiry</h2><p>Veylan is fictional. This form creates a brief on this page; it does not send or save your details.</p></div><button class="button accent" type="submit">Preview test brief</button><section class="form-feedback" role="status" tabindex="-1" hidden></section></form></section>''')
page('information','About this concept','Independent fictional aerospace website concept by Triple Four Studio, with photography credits.','''
<section class="information wrap"><h1>About this concept.</h1><div class="information-body"><h2>An independent design study</h2><p>Veylan Aerospace and Sable are fictional brands created for a website concept by Joshua of Triple Four Studio. This work was not commissioned by, affiliated with or endorsed by Keres or any other aerospace or defense company.</p><p>The company story, product descriptions and illustration demonstrate a possible communication approach. They are not claims about an existing aircraft, verified performance or a real engineering programme. No customer relationships, flight results or certifications are implied.</p><h2>Sable product visual</h2><p>The opening aircraft image was generated for this fictional Sable concept. This is a generated still, not actual or generated flight-test video. The overhead product view was generated using the hero aircraft as its design reference. Both views and the simplified flight-map silhouette are creative studies, not validated aircraft designs.</p><h2>Photography</h2><p>Real aircraft photographs establish the aerospace context. The aircraft shown are not Veylan or Sable products, and the photographs do not imply a relationship with their manufacturers or operators.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/fighter-jet-ascending-against-clear-blue-sky-15861613/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aircraft in flight — Pexels, photo 15861613</a></li><li><a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/gray-airplane-in-the-hangar-5550510/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Aircraft maintenance — Pexels, photo 5550510</a></li><li><a href="https://www.pexels.com/photo/details-of-contemporary-airplane-in-hangar-in-sunlight-5493554/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wing and engines — Pexels, photo 5493554</a></li></ul><h2>Flight-test footage</h2><p>Video excerpts show NASA’s X-59 flight testing, filmed June 5, 2026. Source: NASA / Armstrong Flight Research Center; photographer Lori Losey. This footage provides aerospace research context. The X-59 is not a Veylan or Sable aircraft, and NASA does not endorse this concept.</p><p><a href="https://images.nasa.gov/details/AFRC-2026-14622-X-59_Flt17_FSS_B-ROLL_6-5-26_YT" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original NASA footage and metadata</a></p><h2>Typography</h2><p>Barlow Condensed and Manrope are distributed under the SIL Open Font License. The fonts are served locally, with no requests to a third-party font service.</p><h2>Your privacy</h2><p>This preview has no analytics, advertising or tracking cookies. The demonstration enquiry form does not send or persist entered information. The hosting service may process normal technical request data to deliver the website.</p><a class="button" href="/">Back to Veylan</a></div></section>''')
